  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 331 AM EDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis: \n\nBroad split flow-pattern continues to encompass North America today \nwith multiple waves over Canada and broad midlevel ridging over the \nmid to southern U.S. Subsidence aloft will support surface high \npressure today with mostly sunny skies besides a few isolated \nshowers across Eastern Upper supported by weak low level troughing. \nThe only main chance of precipitation for the entirety of the \nforecast period remains on Friday as a wave currently over central \nSaskatchewan progresses eastward. An attached and disheveled surface \nlow lifts across Hudson Bay and provides enough moisture to support \nlight showers with a few embedded thunderstorms to the Great Lakes \nRegion Friday and Saturday\n\nAforementioned midlevel troughing behind the frontal passage will \nreturn below-normal temperatures this weekend followed by highs \nbuilding back to climatological normals this Monday. Broad ridging \nwill return surface high pressure and overall quiet weather through \nthe remainder of the forecast period. \n\nForecast Details:\n\nToday: Some low-level Cu will be observed this morning across \ninterior areas of the CWA but will dissipate by late morning/midday \nwith typical daytime heating processes. While most locations remain \nmostly sunny and dry due to surface high pressure centered over \nSouthern Lake Michigan, a few isolated showers progress across the \nUpper Peninsula this afternoon. Areas that do observe rainfall are \nattempting to overcome the dry low levels, leading to a general \ntrace to a few hundredths expected. Temperatures remain pretty much \non par for late July across Northern Michigan with highs building to \nthe low to mid 80s combined with light west to northwest flow. Cloud \ncover will begin to increase in the afternoon/evening from the \nnorthwest as the approaching wave returns showers this Friday.\n\nFriday, Saturday, and Sunday: Only real chance of measurable \nprecipitation remains on Friday night as a weak frontal boundary \nsweeps across the Great Lakes Region. Latest guidance depicts \nforcing and instability becoming disheveled with time. The first \nwave of showers arrives overnight Thursday into Friday morning with \nlight amounts of QPF. The main wave of showers and storms is \nexpected during the afternoon and evening hours. Latest model runs \nkeep totals across the majority of the CWA between a few hundredths \nand a quarter inch. However, embedded convection with PWATs over an \ninch will likely deliver localized totals of 1.0\u201d to 1.5\u201d or more, \nespecially around the M-32 corridor. The frontal boundary will stall \njust downstream of the CWA as it heads toward the east coast. \nAdditional rainfall will linger into Saturday, but little to no QPF \nis expected after 12Z Saturday. Post frontal  temperatures have a \nCanadian air mass influence Saturday and Sunday \n\nMonday through Wednesday Outlook: Board 500mb ridging re-develops \nnext week, supporting subsidence aloft and overall benign weather. \nTemperatures return back to climatological normals for late \nJuly/early August with highs in the low to mid 80s through the end \nof the forecast period. Long-range ensembles depict broad midlevel \ntroughing over Canada deepening and returning waves of energy to the \nGreat Lakes Region just beyond the end of the forecast period. \nChances of precipitation will likely return to the long-term \nforecast beyond the middle of next week.",
